ended B, and gave reasoning. Agent 2 previously gave crew cooperation and adaptability observations. Now we need to respond. We need to either agree, challenge, or add new perspective. We could either maintain B or shift. Let's evaluate.\n\nOur own notes: Candidate A can anticipate dangerous situations, sees complex connections, excellent spatial vision, very good leadership qualities. Also regarded as show-off, not open to new ideas. No info on stress resilience, decision quality, attention, responsibility. Candidate B: very conscientious, handles stress well, good at assessing weather, excellent computer skills, weak memory for numbers, makes nasty remarks. Candidate C: makes correct decisions quickly, keeps calm in crisis, understands complicated technology, excellent attention skills, difficulty communicating ideas, egocentric, not willing to further education. Candidate D: responds to unexpected events adequately, can concentrate very well, solves problems extremely well, takes responsibility seriously, regarded as know-it-all, hot temper.\n\nWe have already given crew cooperation and adaptability observations. Now we can discuss decision quality, stress resilience, responsibility, etc. Perhaps we can bring up that Candidate C has proven decision quality (makes correct decisions quickly) and stress resilience (keeps calm in crisis) and technical competence (understands complicated technology) and attention (excellent attention skills). However, his crew cooperation issues (communication difficulty, egocentric) and low adaptability (not willing to further education) are concerns. Candidate D has responsibility and problem solving, but hot temper and know-it-all affect crew cooperation. Candidate A has leadership and anticipation, but no evidence on stress resilience or decision quality.\n\nThus we might argue that Candidate C's strengths in decision quality and stress resilience are critical, perhaps outweighing his interpersonal deficits, which could be mitigated with training. Or we could argue that B's strengths are broader and his crew cooperation issue is less severe than C's egocentric and communication difficulty.\n\nWe need to decide our recommendation. As Agent 2, we haven't voted yet. We can either align with B or propose another candidate. Let's evaluate: B's reliability and stress handling are good, but his crew cooperation is poor (nasty remarks). C's decision quality and stress resilience are strong, but crew cooperation also poor (communication difficulty, egocentric). D's responsibility and problem solving are strong, but crew cooperation issues (know-it-all, hot temper). A's leadership and anticipation are strong, but crew cooperation (show-off) and low adaptability.\n\nAll have crew cooperation issues. Which candidate has the best overall safety-critical profile? B has reliability and stress handling, plus technical competence. C has decision quality and stress resilience, technical competence, attention. D has responsibility and problem solving, concentration. A has leadership and anticipation.\n\nGiven the criteria, reliability and stress resilience are essential. B is very conscientious (reliability) and handles stress well. C also keeps calm in crisis (stress resilience) but we have no explicit reliability evidence. B also has technical competence (weather, computer). C has technical competence (understands complicated technology) and attention. Decision quality is crucial; C makes correct decisions quickly, B no explicit decision quality evidence. Responsibility: D takes responsibility seriously, B no explicit. Leadership: A has very good leadership qualities.\n\nThus B seems balanced but crew cooperation is a problem. However all have issues. Perhaps we can argue that B's crew cooperation issue is more severe (nasty remarks) than others? C's communication difficulty is also severe. D's hot temper is severe. A's show-off is also severe. So all similar.\n\nWe could propose Candidate C as a strong alternative due to decision quality and stress resilience, and argue that his technical competence and attention are strong, and his crew cooperation issues could be mitigated. But we need to weigh.\n\nGiven the panel hasn't yet discussed decision quality for B, we could ask for that. But we can also bring up that we have no evidence of B's decision quality under pressure, which is a gap. That could weaken B. Meanwhile we have evidence that C makes correct decisions quickly, which is a strong point. So we could shift to C.\n\nThus as Agent 2, we could say: I see that B lacks evidence on decision quality, while C has clear evidence of quick correct decisions and calmness in crisis, which are vital. Though C has crew cooperation concerns, they are comparable to B's. Therefore I propose Candidate C as the provisional choice.\n\nWe need to reference the discussion: Agent 3 and Agent 1 recommended B; we note lack of decision quality evidence for B; we highlight C's strengths. Provide explicit facts: C makes correct decisions quickly, keeps calm in crisis, understands complicated technology, excellent attention skills. Also note C's crew cooperation issues (difficulty communicating, egocentric, not willing to further education). Then argue that despite these, his decision quality and stress resilience are critical.\n\nThus we can change our recommendation to Candidate C.\n\nWe need to produce METADATA_JSON with vote \"Candidate C\". Provide public message with reasoning.\n\nLet's do that."}
